.Dd July 21, 2026
.Dt RZ_AX 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm rz-ax
.Nd rizin base converter
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm rz-ax
.Op Fl abBdDeEfFhHiIklLmnNopqrsStuvwWx
.Op expr ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
This command is part of the Rizin project.
.Pp
This command allows you to convert values between positive and
negative integer, float, octal, binary, ternary, and hexadecimal values.
It also evaluates arithmetic expressions written in the RzNum
expression language - the same one used throughout Rizin - with
operators, fixed-width bit-vectors, arbitrary-precision big numbers,
and built-in functions.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Fl
.It Fl a
Show ASCII table
.It Fl b
Convert from binary to string (rz-ax -b 01000101 01110110)
.It Fl B
Convert from string to binary (rz-ax -B hello)
.It Fl d
Force output as integer (rz-ax -d 3 outputs 3 instead of 0x3)
.It Fl D
Base64 decode
.It Fl e
Swap endianness (rz-ax -e 0x33)
.It Fl E
Base64 encode
.It Fl f
Floating point arithmetic (rz-ax -f 6.3+2.1)
.It Fl F
Read C strings from stdin and output in hexpairs.
Useful to load shellcodes (rz-ax -F < shellcode.[c/py/js])
.It Fl h
Show the short help: the usage line and the option list
.It Fl H
Show the long help: everything in
.Fl h
plus the conversion table, expression examples, the operator list and the
literal forms
.It Fl i
Dump stdin to C array in stdout (rz-ax -i < bytes)
.It Fl I
Convert LONG to/from IP ADDRESS (rz-ax -I 3530468537)
.It Fl k
Keep the same base as the input data (rz-ax -k 33+3 -> 36)
.It Fl l
Append newline to the decoded output
.It Fl L
Convert binary to hexadecimal (rz-ax -L 111111111 outputs 0x1ff)
.It Fl n
Show hexpairs from integer value (rz-ax -n 0x1234 # 34120000)
.It Fl N
Show hex C string from integer value (rz-ax -N 0x1234 # \ex34\ex12\ex00\ex00)
.It Fl o
Convert from octal string to char (rz-ax -o \e162 \e172 # rz)
.It Fl r
Rz style output (rz-ax -r 0x1234)
.It Fl s
Convert from hex string to character (rz-ax -s 43 4a 50)
.It Fl S
Convert from character to hex string (rz-ax -S < /bin/ls > ls.hex)
.It Fl t
Convert Unix timestamp to string (rz-ax -t 1234567890)
.It Fl m
Convert MS-DOS timestamp to string (rz-ax -m 0x12345678)
.It Fl W
Convert Win32 timestamp to string (rz-ax -W 1234567890)
.It Fl u
Convert given value to human readable units format (rz-ax -u 389289238 # 371.3M)
.It Fl v
Show version information
.It Fl w
Convert signed word (rz-ax -w 16 0xffff)
.It Fl x
Convert a string into a hash (rz-ax -x linux osx)
.It Fl p
Show position of set bits in the binary representation (rz-ax -p 0xb3)
.El
.Sh OUTPUT BASE
By default a bare literal written in a non-decimal base prints as decimal
and everything else - decimal literals and every compound expression -
prints as hexadecimal.
.Pp
  $ rz-ax 3
  0x3
  $ rz-ax 0x12121212
  303174162
  $ rz-ax 0b1010
  10
  $ rz-ax 0x3+0b11+3
  0x9
.Pp
Prefix an argument with
.Cm =
to force the output base for the arguments that follow it:
.Pp
  =2    binary
  =3    ternary
  =8    octal
  =10   decimal
  =16   hexadecimal
.Pp
.Fl d
forces base 10 and
.Fl k
keeps the input base.
.Sh CONVERSIONS
  int     \->  hex     rz-ax 10
  hex     \->  int     rz-ax 0xa
  \-int    \->  hex     rz-ax \-77
  \-hex    \->  int     rz-ax 0xffffffb3
  int     \->  bin     rz-ax b30
  int     \->  ternary rz-ax t42
  int     \->  oct     rz-ax Ox12 (O is a letter)
  bin     \->  int     rz-ax 0b1010
  oct     \->  int     rz-ax 0o17
  ternary \->  int     rz-ax 0t212
  float   \->  hex     rz-ax 3.33f
  hex     \->  float   rz-ax Fx40551ed8
  hex     \->  bin     rz-ax Bx63
  hex     \->  ternary rz-ax Tx23
  raw     \->  hex     rz-ax \-S < /binfile
  hex     \->  raw     rz-ax \-s 414141
.Pp
The trailing base suffixes (101b, 35o, 212t) are deprecated in favour of
the 0b, 0o and 0t prefixes;
.Nm
still accepts them but prints a warning.
.Sh EXPRESSION SYNTAX
Arguments are evaluated in the RzNum expression language, the same one
used by the rizin
.Cm %
command and by every command argument that takes a number.
Quote an expression containing spaces so it stays a single argument;
otherwise each argument is converted on its own.
.Pp
  $ rz-ax '3 * (10 + 2)'
  0x24
  $ rz-ax 10 0x20
  0xa
  32
.Ss Operators
Listed from highest to lowest precedence; the precedence matches C.
.Pp
  **                power (2**10); note ^ is XOR, not power
  * / %             multiply, divide, modulo
  sdiv smod         signed divide, signed modulo
  + \-               add, subtract (unary \- negates)
  << >>             logical shift left, right
  sar               arithmetic (sign-extending) shift right
  <<< >>>           rotate left, right
  & | ^ ~           and, or, xor, not
  < <= > >= == !=   comparisons, yielding 0 or 1
  ?:                ternary (cond ? a : b)
  ;                 sequence; the value is that of the last expression
.Ss Literals
  0x1f              hexadecimal
  0o17              octal
  0t212             ternary
  0b1010            binary
  017               C style octal (a leading zero before a digit)
  3.5  1e3  0x1.8p3 floating point, including hex floats
  4KiB 2MB          size units: KiB MiB GiB TiB PiB EiB and KB MB GB TB PB EB
  "AB"              a string, taken as its bytes
.Ss Bit-vectors
A
.Cm u
suffix followed by a bit count makes a fixed-width bit-vector, which is
printed with its width and bit pattern instead of as a plain number.
Any width from 1 to 65536 is accepted.
.Pp
  $ rz-ax 8u7
  hex     0x08
  binary  0b0001000
  width   7 bits
  $ rz-ax 5u8+3u8
  hex     0x08
  binary  0b00001000
  width   8 bits
.Ss Big numbers
A result that does not fit in 64 bits is promoted to an
arbitrary-precision integer automatically; no suffix is needed.
.Pp
  $ rz-ax 2**64
  decimal 18446744073709551616
.Ss Functions
  abs  ceil  clz  ctz  floor  gcd  len  ln  log  log10  log2
  max  min  popcount  round  sqrt
.Pp
  $ rz-ax 'gcd(12,18)'
  0x6
  $ rz-ax 'popcount(0xff)'
  0x8
.Ss Variables
An assignment binds a name for the rest of the expression.
.Pp
  $ rz-ax 'x=7;x*x'
  0x31
.Ss Typed address dereference
When a host supplies an IO backend - inside rizin, not in standalone
.Nm -
a numeric address followed by a colon and a width reads memory at that
address:
.Pp
  0x1234:le32       little-endian unsigned 32-bit
  0xDEADBEEF:be64   big-endian unsigned 64-bit
  0x1000:8          native-endian unsigned 8-bit
  0x1000:s32        native-endian signed 32-bit
  0x1000:lef32      little-endian 32-bit float
  0x1000:f16        native-endian half-precision float
.Pp
The width is one of 8, 16, 32, 64 or 128 for integer reads and 16, 32 or
64 for float reads;
.Cm le
or
.Cm be
selects the endianness and
.Cm s
marks a signed read.
.Sh STANDARD INPUT
With no argument,
.Nm
reads one expression per line from standard input.
.Pp
  $ printf '33\n0x41\n' | rz-ax
  0x21
  65
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits 0 when every argument was converted, and 1 when any of them failed
to parse or evaluate.
A diagnostic naming the failure is printed on standard error.
.Pp
  $ rz-ax 'nosuchthing'
  rz-ax: cannot resolve 'nosuchthing'
  $ echo $?
  1
.Sh EXAMPLES
Unpack a hexpair encoded string:
.Pp
  $ rz-ax \-s 41 42 43
  ABC
.Pp
Get the byte encoding of a value:
.Pp
  $ rz-ax \-N 0x1234
  \ex34\ex12\ex00\ex00
.Pp
Find which bits are set:
.Pp
  $ rz-ax \-p 0xb3
  [0\-1]: 1
  [4\-5]: 1
  [7]: 1
.Pp
Convert a value to human readable units:
.Pp
  $ rz-ax \-u 389289238
  371.3M
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr rizin 1 ,
.Xr rz-asm 1 ,
.Xr rz-bin 1 ,
.Xr rz-diff 1 ,
.Xr rz-find 1 ,
.Xr rz-gg 1 ,
.Xr rz-hash 1 ,
